### How does Avro work?

Avro stores data row by row, keeping all of one record's fields together, and writes the full schema in the file header in JSON. Writing a record means appending it whole, which is efficient for streaming ingestion. The embedded schema plus well-defined compatibility rules make schema evolution a first-class feature.

```java
package com.bank.formats;

public class AvroOverview {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Avro layout (row-based):");
        System.out.println("  Header: {schema JSON}");
        System.out.println("  Record 1: [T1|SAV2001|15000|INR]");
        System.out.println("  Record 2: [T2|CUR3002|25000|INR]");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("Strengths:");
        System.out.println("  - Fast writes (append whole record)");
        System.out.println("  - Schema evolution (add/remove fields)");
        System.out.println("  - Pairs with Schema Registry for Kafka");
        System.out.println("  - Compact binary encoding");
    }
}
```

```text
Avro layout (row-based):
  Header: {schema JSON}
  Record 1: [T1|SAV2001|15000|INR]
  Record 2: [T2|CUR3002|25000|INR]

Strengths:
  - Fast writes (append whole record)
  - Schema evolution (add/remove fields)
  - Pairs with Schema Registry for Kafka
  - Compact binary encoding
```

### How does Parquet work?

Parquet stores data column by column: all values of the amount column together, then all of date, organized into row groups and column chunks. This layout makes column pruning and predicate pushdown possible, and same-type values compress far better.

```java
package com.bank.formats;

public class ParquetOverview {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Parquet layout (columnar):");
        System.out.println("  txn_id column:  [T1, T2, T3, ...]");
        System.out.println("  amount column:  [15000, 25000, ...]");
        System.out.println("  date column:    [Oct-29, Oct-29, ...]");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("Strengths:");
        System.out.println("  - Column pruning (read only needed cols)");
        System.out.println("  - Predicate pushdown (min/max skip)");
        System.out.println("  - Strong compression (same-type values)");
        System.out.println("  - Default for Spark, Trino, cloud lakes");
    }
}
```

```text
Parquet layout (columnar):
  txn_id column:  [T1, T2, T3, ...]
  amount column:  [15000, 25000, ...]
  date column:    [Oct-29, Oct-29, ...]

Strengths:
  - Column pruning (read only needed cols)
  - Predicate pushdown (min/max skip)
  - Strong compression (same-type values)
  - Default for Spark, Trino, cloud lakes
```

### How does ORC work?

ORC is also columnar with the same analytical advantages, but it adds richer built-in indexes and statistics at file, stripe, and row-group levels, including optional bloom filters. Its standout feature is first-class Hive ACID transaction support.

```java
package com.bank.formats;

public class OrcOverview {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("ORC layout (columnar, Hive-optimized):");
        System.out.println("  Similar to Parquet: column-oriented");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("ORC extras vs Parquet:");
        System.out.println("  - Richer built-in indexes and stats");
        System.out.println("  - Built-in bloom filters");
        System.out.println("  - First-class Hive ACID support");
        System.out.println("  - Row-level UPDATE/DELETE in Hive");
    }
}
```

```text
ORC layout (columnar, Hive-optimized):
  Similar to Parquet: column-oriented

ORC extras vs Parquet:
  - Richer built-in indexes and stats
  - Built-in bloom filters
  - First-class Hive ACID support
  - Row-level UPDATE/DELETE in Hive
```

### How do the three formats compare?

| Aspect | Avro | Parquet | ORC |
|---|---|---|---|
| Layout | Row-based | Columnar | Columnar |
| Schema | In file header (JSON) | In file footer | In file tail |
| Schema evolution | First-class | Supported | Supported |
| Column pruning | No (reads all cols) | Yes | Yes |
| Predicate pushdown | No | Yes (min/max) | Yes (indexes) |
| Compression | Good | Very good | Very good |
| ACID | No | No (needs Delta) | Yes (Hive) |
| Best for | Writes, Kafka, ingestion | Reads, analytics, Spark | Hive, ACID analytics |

```java
package com.bank.formats;

public class FormatComparison {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Avro:    write-heavy, schema evolution, Kafka");
        System.out.println("Parquet: read-heavy, analytics, Spark/Trino");
        System.out.println("ORC:     Hive ecosystem, ACID updates");
    }
}
```

```text
Avro:    write-heavy, schema evolution, Kafka
Parquet: read-heavy, analytics, Spark/Trino
ORC:     Hive ecosystem, ACID updates
```

### Why not analyze directly on Avro?

A columnar scan touching a few columns of a wide table reads a tiny fraction of the bytes, while Avro must read whole rows. On a 100-column table, Parquet reads only the 2 needed columns; Avro reads all 100.

```java
package com.bank.formats;

public class WhyNotAvroForAnalytics {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("SUM(amount) on 100-column table:");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("Avro:    reads ALL 100 columns per row.");
        System.out.println("         I/O: 10 GB");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("Parquet: reads ONLY amount column.");
        System.out.println("         I/O: 100 MB");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("100x less I/O with Parquet.");
    }
}
```

```text
SUM(amount) on 100-column table:

Avro:    reads ALL 100 columns per row.
         I/O: 10 GB

Parquet: reads ONLY amount column.
         I/O: 100 MB

100x less I/O with Parquet.
```

### Why not stream events into Parquet?

Columnar formats want large batched writes. Streaming individual events into Parquet produces a small-files problem: thousands of tiny files that are inefficient to read. Use Avro for streaming and convert to Parquet in a batch compaction step.

```java
package com.bank.formats;

public class WhyNotParquetForStreaming {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Streaming 1 event at a time into Parquet:");
        System.out.println("  Creates 1 tiny file per event.");
        System.out.println("  1M events = 1M files.");
        System.out.println("  Reading: slow (file open overhead).");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("Solution: two-stage pipeline");
        System.out.println("  1. Stream events -> Avro (row-friendly)");
        System.out.println("  2. Batch compact -> Parquet (col-friendly)");
    }
}
```

```text
Streaming 1 event at a time into Parquet:
  Creates 1 tiny file per event.
  1M events = 1M files.
  Reading: slow (file open overhead).

Solution: two-stage pipeline
  1. Stream events -> Avro (row-friendly)
  2. Batch compact -> Parquet (col-friendly)
```

### When do you use each format?

Use Avro for the write and ingestion path: streaming events, Kafka messages, and anywhere the schema evolves. Use Parquet for the analytical read path: curated tables scanned by reports. Use ORC in the Hive ecosystem when ACID updates are needed. Modern lakehouses (Delta, Iceberg) layer transactions over Parquet, reducing the need for ORC-on-Hive.

```java
package com.bank.formats;

public class WhenToUseEach {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("National Bank two-stage pipeline:");
        System.out.println("  1. Kafka -> Avro (streaming, evolving)");
        System.out.println("  2. ETL -> Parquet (curated analytics)");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("Hive regulatory table needing corrections:");
        System.out.println("  -> ORC with Hive ACID");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("Modern alternative to ORC-on-Hive:");
        System.out.println("  -> Delta Lake or Iceberg on Parquet");
    }
}
```

```text
National Bank two-stage pipeline:
  1. Kafka -> Avro (streaming, evolving)
  2. ETL -> Parquet (curated analytics)

Hive regulatory table needing corrections:
  -> ORC with Hive ACID

Modern alternative to ORC-on-Hive:
  -> Delta Lake or Iceberg on Parquet
```

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

- How does Avro schema evolution work differently from Parquet?
- What is the small-files problem and how do you solve it?
- How do Delta Lake and Iceberg provide ACID on Parquet without ORC?
- When would you choose ORC over Parquet today?
- What is the role of a Schema Registry with Avro in Kafka?
